{"author":{"id":"dec876858d28babbadcead4137861bf586626b8518cbce9c5f4fc52ac3b424b8"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1790966794,"nonce":"sgGxAU7llWXGZDBUtUau1pOXSNY=","title":"Recompiling into a live BlincDsl re-runs the changed module but the entry keeps calling the","message":"**Symptom.** Adding a widget to an imported `.blinc` module fires a re-render\nand changes nothing on screen. Editing the ENTRY file appears to work, which\nmakes it look like a watcher or invalidation problem.\n\n**Cause.** Two stacked, and the first hides the second:\n\n1. `compile_project` skips any module already in `compiled_modules` -- that is\n   what stops a diamond import compiling twice. On a reload it also stops\n   anything but the entry recompiling.\n2. Even with that cleared, compiling into a LIVE runtime re-runs the module but\n   the entry's call to `\u003cModule\u003e$view` keeps binding to the symbol registered\n   the first time. New code, old binding.\n\n**Fix (531c54c4).** `BlincDsl::reload_project(entry, root, setup)` builds a\nFRESH instance, runs `setup` (where the host re-registers extern widgets -- a\nnew runtime knows none of them), then compiles. The caller swaps it in; on\nfailure it keeps the instance it has, which is what makes editing a file\ncharacter by character survivable. A fresh instance starts with empty\naccumulators, so the clear/restore juggling an in-place recompile needed goes\naway too.\n\n**Why state survives.** Signal values live in a process-global registry keyed\nby name, and a declared default applies only when a signal is first minted\n(b1fddeb0), so the new instance adopts what the old one was showing. Pinned by\n`crates/blinc_dsl_core/tests/hot_reload_contract.rs`.\n\n**Host wiring.** `BlincDsl` owns JIT function pointers and is NOT `Send`, so\nthe rebuild cannot happen on the watcher thread. The watcher raises a flag;\nthe swap happens at the top of the UI builder, on the main thread, before\nanything reads the program. `blinc_app::hot_reload::watch_dir_with` runs a\ncallback before queueing the rebuild, and `request_rebuild` exists for callers\nthat regenerate the UI outside the builder.\n\n**Diagnosis that worked.** A headless two-file project: compile, edit only the\nmodule, reload, count nodes. 3 -\u003e 3 said the module never reached the view;\nbreaking the module and seeing the reload fail proved it WAS being compiled,\nwhich isolated the fault to symbol binding rather than compilation.\n\nSee [[project_dsl_hot_reload_plan.md]], project multi module blinc plan.","files":null}]}